  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "R7 provides the clearest precedent for controlled in situ heating of an equiatomic HEA through a comparable temperature range under vacuum and reports selective elemental depletion, while R10 shows segregation in a nanostructured multicomponent alloy. R1 supports appreciable high-temperature lattice diffusion, R5 connects surface free energy and microstructure to high-temperature surface reactions, and R8, R12, and R14 provide weaker precedents for temperature- or excitation-driven redistribution, coalescence, and preferential elemental loss. However, none identifies Au/Cu surface segregation in AuCoCuNiPt nanoparticles, links it specifically to low surface energies and shape-dependent diffusion, or predicts nanoparticle disappearance and Au/Cu evaporation. Thus the records point toward thermal redistribution under vacuum but leave the specific nanoparticle intervention and mechanism as a substantial new step."
